    pnPhGateHead: "What this pH means for what is growing here",
    pnPhWhyLow: "soil pH {ph} is {short} below this plant's floor of {floor}",
    pnPhWhyHigh: "soil pH {ph} is {over} above this plant's ceiling of {ceiling}, and it needs acid soil: above its range iron stops being available and the leaves yellow while the veins stay green",
    pnPhWhyKit: " (a home kit resolves about +/-{n}, so this is marginal)",
    pnPhGateFix: "Lime raises pH and sulfur lowers it. We can't tell you how much of either, because the amount is your lab's to give: ask it for its lime or sulfur recommendation for your own soil.",
    pnPhGateFloors: "These floors are estimates too. Each marks the pH at which the plant's own source says to lime (for most vegetables, University of Maryland Extension's table), and a plant can still live below it.",
    pnPhGateCeilings: "This ceiling is an estimate too. The sources we read for an acid-loving plant disagree on where it sits, and agree only that it has one.",
    pnPhGateCeilingAlone: "This ceiling is an estimate. The sources we read for an acid-loving plant disagree on where it sits, and agree only that it has one.",
    pnPhGateOk: "Nothing growing here sits below its floor at pH {ph}.",
    pnPhGateCacl2: "Your pH of {ph} was measured in calcium chloride. The plant ranges here are in water pH, and the gap between the two changes from one soil to the next, so we've saved this reading and haven't checked your plants against it. If your lab can give you the pH in water too, add it and we'll check them.",
    pnPhGateOtherSalt: "Your pH of {ph} was measured in a salt solution. The plant ranges here are in water pH, so we've saved this reading and haven't checked your plants against it. If your lab can give you the pH in water too, add it and we'll check them.",
    pnPhGateUnsure: "We've saved your pH of {ph}. Your report says which pH it is, usually as pH (water) or pH (CaCl2). The two are often half a unit to a unit apart, so we'll check your plants once you tell us.",
    pnLimeHead: "What liming this ground means for what is growing here",
    pnLimeWhy: "this ground was limed{when}{tail}: common scab worsens as soil pH rises, and this plant wants it at or below about {max}",
    pnLimeWhyOn: " on {date}",
    pnLimeWhyPh: ", and the pH {ph} read on or after that day is above {max}",
    pnLimeFix: "Scab is skin-deep, and the tubers are still good to eat. Where it has been a problem, put the potatoes on ground you have not limed, choose a scab-resistant variety, keep the soil evenly moist for the four to nine weeks after planting while the tubers set, and never lime or add wood ash where potatoes are going next. A later pH reading at or below about {ph} here would clear this warning. We won't give a sulfur rate to get there. That amount is your lab's to give: ask it for its sulfur recommendation for your soil.",
    pnClubHead: "What this pH means on ground that has carried brassicas",
    pnClubWhy: "this ground carried brassicas within the last {years} years and its pH {ph} sits {short} below {min}: clubroot's resting spores germinate in acid soil, and raising the pH is the lever every clubroot guide we read names",
    pnClubFix: "Raise this ground's pH toward {ph} with lime well ahead of planting. Use calcitic lime, or dolomitic if your magnesium is low. Once the pH is up, watch for boron shortage on coarse soils. The amount of lime comes from your lab, from a lime requirement test of your soil. Rotation stays the first lever; this is the second.",
